---
title: "The reader experience"
description: "What a built page gives its reader: a page lit by their own device, keyboard access, and a record of the reader chrome that was tried and then cut."
---

A Taliesin page is not just rendered HTML, it is a reading surface. What it gives a reader
needs no author configuration and, as of 2026-08-13, no reader configuration either.

One principle holds for everything on this page: **reader-side and read-only.** Nothing here
is written back to your `.tmd` source and nothing leaves the browser. This is the
single-editing-surface rule applied to the output: the reader owns a reading of the
document, the document stays authoritative.

## Theme

**The page is lit by the reader's device, and there is no control.** A phone set to dark
renders the page dark; a laptop set to light renders it light; flipping that setting mid-read
flips the page live. Both palettes ship inside every page and one is selected *before the
page paints*, so there is no flash of the wrong theme and no round trip.

There was a Settings gear in the top-right corner offering Auto / Light / Dark, and it was
removed. The reader had already answered the question once, in their operating system, and
every other page they read that day honoured that answer. A per-site picker asks them to
answer it a second time, stores that answer where only this site can see it, and then
disagrees with the rest of their screen. Auto was the default and was what almost everyone
kept.

**Two themes, not three.** A warm low-contrast "sepia" mode shipped for a while and was
removed: two themes cover the two conditions that actually differ, a lit room and a dark
one, and each is tuned to clear its contrast floors. A third palette is a third of
everything, every rule and every contrast measurement, for a preference the reader can
get from their own system or browser. An author who wants a warm page ships it as a
custom `theme:` — a `.css`/`.scss` file, or an `_extensions/` theme bundle.

There is deliberately no text-size, width, or spacing knob. Taliesin tunes those defaults
once, for every reader, rather than shipping a comfort panel of sliders: the browser
already offers page zoom for the rare reader who needs larger type, and one well-set
measure beats a pile of controls nobody opens.

## No focus mode, and no fullscreen

A reading page has neither a focus/reading mode nor a fullscreen toggle, and that is a
decision rather than a gap. Both were shipped and then removed: the chrome a page carries
is a slim topbar or navbar and a footer, all of it static, and static chrome does not
compete for attention the way a moving sidebar or an ad would. Hiding it bought a reader
nothing they could feel, while costing them their navigation and two controls to learn.

A book made the point plainly. Once a chapter's right-rail TOC was removed, focus mode
had literally nothing left to hide there: the sticky topbar was never in its hide list,
so the toggle was a no-op that still occupied a row in the Settings menu of the day.

Nothing replaced it: the browser's own fullscreen (F11) is one key away and belongs
to the reader, not to the document.

## What else was tried, and cut

This page is also a record. Each of the following existed, worked, and was still
removed — not from neglect, but because it cost more chrome than it repaid. Read this
before asking for one of them back:

- **Hover cross-reference cards.** Hovering a citation or a `@fig-`/`@sec-`/`@eq-`
  cross-reference used to pop a preview card, uninvited, on passive mouse movement — the
  single most intrusive thing on a reading page. The reference still resolves and still
  navigates; only the passive preview is gone.
- **Reading-position resume.** A pill used to scroll a returning reader back to the exact
  block they left, anchored to its content hash. Browsers already restore scroll position
  on reload and on back-navigation, so the pill was a second, less reliable copy of an
  answer the reader already had.
- **TOC read checkmarks.** Sections you had scrolled past used to gain a checkmark in the
  table of contents, animating in response to nothing the reader had asked for.
- **Anchor copy-links.** Hovering a heading or a figure used to reveal a `#` to copy its
  link. The table of contents already emits the same deep links, so the control duplicated
  one that already existed.
- **The mobile "Contents" pull-up sheet.** A labelled button used to open the table of
  contents as a bottom sheet on a phone. It duplicated the topbar, which is already sticky
  and already carries navigation; the table of contents now simply lists after the
  article on a narrow screen.
- **The image lightbox.** Clicking a figure used to open it full-screen. Browsers already
  open an image in a new tab and pinch-zoom it natively, and the built-in viewer cost 292
  lines plus a permanently armed click handler on every figure. Known loss: a complex
  diagram loses its comfortable mobile inspection path — accepted, revisit only on a real
  reader report.
- **The show/hide-code toggle.** A reader-side switch used to collapse every executed
  cell's source. The author already decides per cell with `#| echo:`; a reader override of
  that decision is real but rare, and it cost a permanent row in the Settings menu.
- **The code-download box.** A page with code cells used to end with an aside offering
  every cell's source as one script per language, carried in the page as a `data:` URL.
  It was well built — it read the block model, so a `#| echo: false` cell was in the file,
  and it declined to offer `{js}`, whose order is its dependency graph rather than the
  page. It was still permanent chrome on every computational page, answering a question
  most readers never ask, and the source it handed over is already on the page and in
  view-source. An author who wants the reader to have a runnable script links one.
- **The theme picker, and the Settings gear that held it.** A segmented Auto / Light / Dark
  control, described above. Removing it emptied the gear, so the gear went too, and with it
  the last reader-facing control on a built page. A third door had to close in the same
  change: the Cmd-K palette carried a "Toggle light / dark theme" command that gated on a
  global every page ships, so unlike its neighbours it appeared on static builds as well.
- **Listing category-filter chips.** A filter row used to sit above a listing's cards. It
  paid off only on a large archive with a disciplined category vocabulary; page-level
  `categories:` and the card badges on each listing still work.
- **The `?` and `/` character-key shortcuts.** Bare-letter shortcuts used to open the
  settings menu and search, which under WCAG 2.1.4 legally required a switch to turn them
  off. Removing
  two rarely used shortcuts removed the obligation rather than adding the switch; `Esc`
  and the arrow keys below are not character keys and stay live with no control needed.

## Keyboard and accessibility

Tab from the top of any page reveals a **Skip to content** link that jumps focus past
the chrome straight to the prose. The link and its focusable `<main>` target are
emitted server-side, so they work even with JavaScript disabled. A few keyboard
interactions speed up reading (each is ignored while you are typing in a field or a
dialog is open):

- <kbd>Cmd/Ctrl-K</kbd> opens the search palette.
- `←` / `→` move to the previous / next chapter (in a book).
- `Esc` closes the open menu, dialog, or palette.

None of these is a character-key shortcut (a single key fired with no modifier), so
WCAG 2.1.4's requirement to make character-key shortcuts switchable off does not apply,
and no off-switch is owed.

Keyboard focus is always visible: every interactive control (links, buttons, and tabs)
shares one consistent focus ring, shown only for keyboard or assistive-technology focus and
never on a mouse click. The ring is a single theme token, so it follows the active palette
(including the high-contrast modes below).

**Landmarks and dialogs.** Each `<nav>` on a page carries a distinguishing accessible
name (the table of contents announces as "Table of contents", separate from the
navbar and the prev/next pager), so a screen reader's landmark list is navigable.
The one modal overlay, the **search palette**, moves focus into itself on open, traps
Tab while open, and restores focus to whatever opened it on close.

**High contrast.** In Windows High Contrast / `forced-colors` mode, and under
`prefers-contrast: more`, borders, focus rings, separators, and the active-item
markers are re-asserted with system colors so the chrome stays legible when the
palette is forced.

Prose also gets quiet typographic polish that needs no configuration: balanced
headings and captions, pretty last-line wrapping, and figures that keep their caption
attached.
